hip-hop into your wine names

00:10:01.944 --> 00:10:03.522
and things of that nature, right?

00:10:03.522 --> 00:10:04.918
- Yeah, absolutely, I mean, you know,

00:10:04.918 --> 00:10:07.457
I'm just a kid who, I grew up on hip-hop,

00:10:07.457 --> 00:10:09.906
and I just happened to
fall in love with wine,

00:10:09.906 --> 00:10:10.739
and I just kept thinking,

00:10:10.739 --> 00:10:12.921
why not be able to put
those two things together?

00:10:12.921 --> 00:10:15.384
It felt like every person I talked to

00:10:15.384 --> 00:10:16.636
who was in the wine business,

00:10:16.636 --> 00:10:18.682
always got the hip-hop references,

00:10:18.682 --> 00:10:20.359
we're always singing those songs.

00:10:20.359 --> 00:10:22.276
- [Erin] I'd love that.

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:27.147
- As a Texas boy, I feel
like Scarface hasn't entered

00:10:27.147 --> 00:10:29.370
any of your labels just yet.

00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:30.902
Don't you think that's
a little disrespectful?

00:10:30.902 --> 00:10:34.285
And can we please address that
with one of your next cuvees?

00:10:34.285 --> 00:10:36.796
- Maybe we'll do it subtly, but,

00:10:36.796 --> 00:10:38.311
they were raw, they were raw.

00:10:38.311 --> 00:10:40.981
The ghetto boys in Scarface,
they were really raw.

00:10:40.981 --> 00:10:42.498
I don't know if they're
really ready for that.

00:10:42.498 --> 00:10:45.224
I think I, tentatively I
had a wine called Ill Na Na,

00:10:45.224 --> 00:10:47.943
and some others, that hit
the kibosh on that one, so.

00:10:47.943 --> 00:10:49.404
- What, the alcohol and tobacco world

00:10:49.404 --> 00:10:50.237
wouldn't let you do that?

00:10:50.237 --> 00:10:52.723
- No, no, no, I think it was
more my wife was like, dude.

00:10:52.723 --> 00:10:54.461
- You're pushing it too far?

00:10:54.461 --> 00:10:55.913
- I think I know what that is, like--

00:10:55.913 --> 00:10:57.127
- [Gary] Like, cut that shit.
- Yeah, totally.

00:10:57.127 --> 00:10:59.922
- All right, we've
established the four of us.

00:10:59.922 --> 00:11:01.313
Let's go into the first wine.

00:11:01.313 --> 00:11:03.862
Andre, I'm gonna let you take it away.

00:11:03.862 --> 00:11:06.293
What should everybody
be doing at this point?

00:11:06.293 --> 00:11:09.347
- Oh wow, you should be
actually looking at the color.

00:11:09.347 --> 00:11:11.716
Color can tell you a
lot of different things,

00:11:11.716 --> 00:11:12.827
but we have a brown background,

00:11:12.827 --> 00:11:14.612
so maybe you wanna hold it over the rug

00:11:14.612 --> 00:11:15.720
or maybe a white background.

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:18.397
You just wanna kinda look at the wine.

00:11:18.397 --> 00:11:20.347
I mean, we get points for
naming the color of the wine,

00:11:20.347 --> 00:11:22.566
so, it's white, right? (laughs)

00:11:22.566 --> 00:11:24.389
We know that, we got that right,

00:11:24.389 --> 00:11:26.154
take all credit--
- You are crushing it, Andre.

00:11:26.154 --> 00:11:26.987
- What do they say?

00:11:26.987 --> 00:11:28.401
Hey, you get five points
for writing your name

00:11:28.401 --> 00:11:29.309
on the paper, right?
- [Woman] He's the expert?

00:11:29.309 --> 00:11:30.859
- It's true, it's true, so look,

00:11:30.859 --> 00:11:32.339
let's establish some rules here.

00:11:32.339 --> 00:11:35.417
We don't need to guess what
this is, or what you have.

00:11:35.417 --> 00:11:37.360
Where I want to take
it for all four of us,

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:39.229
even for us, like, we don't have to guess

00:11:39.229 --> 00:11:41.247
what regions it's from, what vintage.

00:11:41.247 --> 00:11:43.522
At this point, Andre, it
must be fun for you as well.

00:11:43.522 --> 00:11:45.752
Like, we grew up in the wine
world when it wasn't cultural.

00:11:45.752 --> 00:11:48.281
Now, because of Netflix and
all these documentaries,

00:11:48.281 --> 00:11:50.840
everybody, it feels, has seen Somm

00:11:50.840 --> 00:11:52.884
or this, that, or the other thing, so,

00:11:52.884 --> 00:11:56.244
even though Andre very
much has those capabilities

00:11:56.244 --> 00:11:58.198
and I'm sure guests that
I have on Brown Bags

00:11:58.198 --> 00:11:59.345
will have those capabilities,

00:11:59.345 --> 00:12:01.490
that is not really where
I'm looking to take it.

00:12:01.490 --> 00:12:03.217
Where I'm looking to take it is,

00:12:03.217 --> 00:12:05.768
do you like it, what does it taste like,

00:12:05.768 --> 00:12:09.185
and maybe, how much do you
think it costs, would be cool.

00:12:09.185 --> 00:12:10.690
Can you do me a favor, Trouty,

00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:11.610
I know you're in the background.

00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:12.663
I don't know what the wines are either.

00:12:12.663 --> 00:12:14.259
I know Brandon picked
them from Wine Library.

00:12:14.259 --> 00:12:17.041
Can you get him to text
you what the wines are,

00:12:17.041 --> 00:12:21.655
and maybe the current mainstream
scores and the price point?

00:12:21.655 --> 00:12:23.292
Just so when we have the unveil,

00:12:23.292 --> 00:12:25.484
because I'm totally not prepared for this.

00:12:25.484 --> 00:12:26.534
- [Erin] Can we guess, like--

00:12:26.534 --> 00:12:27.846
- You can guess anything you want.

00:12:27.846 --> 00:12:29.920
- [Erin] I would like to know,
like, without telling us,

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:31.763
I wanna know if we know.

00:12:31.763 --> 00:12:33.319
- Yeah, of course.
- If it's like, a Syrah

00:12:33.319 --> 00:12:34.152
or a Pinot or--

00:12:34.152 --> 00:12:35.491
- [Gary] Yeah, I promise
you it's not any of those,

00:12:35.491 --> 00:12:36.599
since those are red wines.

00:12:36.599 --> 00:12:38.817
- I didn't wanna say
- I'm kidding, I'm kidding,

00:12:38.817 --> 00:12:39.650
I'm kidding.
- whites because,

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:41.363
no, I, because, you know,
anchoring, you know.

00:12:41.363 --> 00:12:42.362
- Yes, no, no, don't worry.

00:12:42.362 --> 00:12:44.018
So, let's sniffy sniff first.

00:12:44.018 --> 00:12:45.497
For anybody who watched Wine Library TV,

00:12:45.497 --> 00:12:48.004
I call it sniffy sniff because
I wanted it to be funny,

00:12:48.004 --> 00:12:51.741
and wanted to be memorable,
but what I really wanted was,

00:12:51.741 --> 00:12:53.379
you know, Wine Library
TV, it'll be interesting.

00:12:53.379 --> 00:12:55.851
I'll actually ask you your
perspective on Wine Library TV.

00:12:55.851 --> 00:12:59.700
It was so important to me
to trick America at scale

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:01.510
into a couple things
that I thought mattered,

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:03.396
which was, one, trust your own palate.

00:13:03.396 --> 00:13:05.536
Forget about what Andre and I say, like,

00:13:05.536 --> 00:13:07.406
we don't get nervous
about our point of view

00:13:07.406 --> 00:13:10.714
on sushi, or a
cheeseburger, but with wine,

00:13:10.714 --> 00:13:12.896
we're always looking at the
person that knows the most,

00:13:12.896 --> 00:13:15.437
and we have to succumb to
his or her point of view.

00:13:15.437 --> 00:13:16.495
I hated that.

00:13:16.495 --> 00:13:18.857
I'd like to change that,
that was a big goal of mine.

00:13:18.857 --> 00:13:21.201
And number two, we need
to smell wine more.

00:13:21.201 --> 00:13:23.581
Like, you guys cannot
imagine how much more

00:13:23.581 --> 00:13:25.898
you will enjoy the wine if you smell it.

00:13:25.898 --> 00:13:27.427
So let's get the sniffy sniff.

00:13:27.427 --> 00:13:28.933
Now, Mary Kate, I don't
want you to be polite

00:13:28.933 --> 00:13:30.182
or PureWow while we're here.

00:13:30.182 --> 00:13:32.963
I want your schnoz, there
you go, all the way in, yeah.

00:13:32.963 --> 00:13:34.144
- That's how you do it?
- It's like, dripping

00:13:34.144 --> 00:13:35.194
in white wine now.
- Yeah, yes.

00:13:35.194 --> 00:13:36.377
- Are you supposed to wait, Gary,

00:13:36.377 --> 00:13:38.015
and let it kind of open
up and keep going back

00:13:38.015 --> 00:13:39.578
and smelling it again and again?

00:13:39.578 --> 00:13:40.924
- Well, I like swirling the wine,

00:13:40.924 --> 00:13:41.874
bringing it up.
- My nose is wet.

00:13:41.874 --> 00:13:43.058
- Yeah, yeah, I learned this.

00:13:43.058 --> 00:13:44.956
- So yeah, you wanna aerate the wine,

00:13:44.956 --> 00:13:45.889
and what Gary's talking about,

00:13:45.889 --> 00:13:47.832
most people don't smell the wine.

00:13:47.832 --> 00:13:49.799
90% of taste is smell, right?

00:13:49.799 --> 00:13:51.003
Think about, like, when you were a kid

00:13:51.003 --> 00:13:52.821
and you pinched your
nose 'cause something,

00:13:52.821 --> 00:13:53.856
you didn't want to eat your asparagus

00:13:53.856 --> 00:13:55.265
or whatever it was, right?

00:13:55.265 --> 00:13:56.098
Kind of the same thing.

00:13:56.098 --> 00:13:58.027
So you want to actually
slightly crack your mouth.

00:13:58.027 --> 00:13:59.595
I'm gettin' a little nerdy
here, Gary, I'm sorry.

00:13:59.595 --> 00:14:00.428
- [Gary] I'm listening.

00:14:00.428 --> 00:14:01.520
- You wanna crack your mouth a little bit,

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:02.698
and you wanna inhale through your nose

00:14:02.698 --> 00:14:04.608
and exhale through your mouth, right?

00:14:04.608 --> 00:14:06.173
And that opens up the olfactory senses,

00:14:06.173 --> 00:14:10.340
so you get, like, this
better sense of smell, yeah.

00:14:11.271 --> 00:14:13.844
So you want, yeah, you wanna smell.

00:14:13.844 --> 00:14:15.182
- [Erin] What you just
said, I've never smelled

00:14:15.182 --> 00:14:16.453
like that before, in my life.

00:14:16.453 --> 00:14:17.728
- Yeah.

00:14:17.728 --> 00:14:20.470
- [Erin] That just blew my mind.

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:21.460
- All right, so what do you
- Are they like tasting?

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:22.596
I'm like spillin' over here.
- guys, what are you guys,

00:14:22.596 --> 00:14:23.429
what'd you get, don't worry.
- Are we tasting

00:14:23.429 --> 00:14:24.408
or not tasting?
- What're you guys getting

00:14:24.408 --> 00:14:25.241
on the nose?

00:14:25.241 --> 00:14:28.232
Like what are we tasting here?

00:14:28.232 --> 00:14:29.627
(sloshing)

00:14:29.627 --> 00:14:31.358
- Are you smelling, are
you hearing me smell?

00:14:31.358 --> 00:14:32.663
- Yes, it's making up

00:14:32.663 --> 00:14:35.129
your smell.
- Oh I thought we were.

00:14:35.129 --> 00:14:36.884
- Erin, I know you're
drunk, and that's fine too.

00:14:36.884 --> 00:14:37.717
(Erin laughing)

00:14:37.717 --> 00:14:39.300
I mean, on the nose thing, like,

00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:41.582
for me, the melon and honeydew thing

00:14:41.582 --> 00:14:42.702
is coming through on the nose,

00:14:42.702 --> 00:14:43.925
which I like.
- I get a little bit of that.

00:14:43.925 --> 00:14:44.894
- What are you getting Andre?
- I get a little bit of

00:14:44.894 --> 00:14:46.404
canned asparagus, like slightly,

00:14:46.404 --> 00:14:47.250
like when you first open it up.

00:14:47.250 --> 00:14:49.357
Not that that's a bad thing
or a good thing, but--

00:14:49.357 --> 00:14:50.408
- You buy canned asparagus?
- This is what I love.

00:14:50.408 --> 00:14:51.274
This is what, exactly.

00:14:51.274 --> 00:14:54.745
This is what always
cracks me about tastings.

00:14:54.745 --> 00:14:56.000
- I know, I--
- No, my mom used to buy it.

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:57.489
- Yeah, I get it, I get it.
- I'm sorry, Mom.

00:14:57.489 --> 00:14:58.359
But my mom used to buy

00:14:58.359 --> 00:14:59.326
canned asparagus.
- Now I assume you go

00:14:59.326 --> 00:15:01.549
to a good, solid, like, you know,

00:15:01.549 --> 00:15:04.488
fresh food farm in Brooklyn.
- Oh you know, feed me

00:15:04.488 --> 00:15:05.321
(mumbles), oh totally, yeah.
- So Gary, I don't know

00:15:05.321 --> 00:15:08.147
if you know this, my
first job out of college,

00:15:08.147 --> 00:15:12.357
I was a whiskey editor, and
I was a 22-year-old girl

00:15:12.357 --> 00:15:14.922
who had never drank
whiskey before in her life.

00:15:14.922 --> 00:15:16.267
And I'd go to these tastings--
- And you got the job

00:15:16.267 --> 00:15:17.285
as the whiskey ed--
- And I got the job.

00:15:17.285 --> 00:15:18.510
- Sounds like a real
fucking fine establishment.

00:15:18.510 --> 00:15:20.135
(laughter)

00:15:20.135 --> 00:15:21.684
- [Mary Kate] The
magazine has since folded.

00:15:21.684 --> 00:15:24.036
- No shit, go ahead.

00:15:24.036 --> 00:15:27.688
- So, no, I edited a very
smart whiskey person,

00:15:27.688 --> 00:15:29.278
but I would have to go on tastings,

00:15:29.278 --> 00:15:31.216
and, Andre, this is when I always wanted

00:15:31.216 --> 00:15:32.951
to call bullshit on people,
because they'd be like,

00:15:32.951 --> 00:15:36.368
"Ah, I smell the pita of the isle of yore

00:15:37.999 --> 00:15:41.395
"in Scotland in 1901."

00:15:41.395 --> 00:15:44.030
I'd be like, what, I smell alcohol.

00:15:44.030 --> 00:15:44.972
- Correct and that's exactly--
- Well that's 'cause

00:15:44.972 --> 00:15:45.805
you didn't know shit.

00:15:45.805 --> 00:15:47.445
- And that's what I
smelled at the beginning.

00:15:47.445 --> 00:15:48.805
Like, when I first started,
- Yeah, me too.

00:15:48.805 --> 00:15:49.638
- it was like--
- Wine.

00:15:49.638 --> 00:15:50.471
- all I smell is alcohol, like,

00:15:50.471 --> 00:15:52.883
I mean, how can you get any
of those things out of that?

00:15:52.883 --> 00:15:53.798
- [Mary Kate] But, canned asparagus,

00:15:53.798 --> 00:15:56.735
this really hearkens you back
to a canned asparagus moment?

00:15:56.735 --> 00:15:58.827
- But these are all moments, right?

00:15:58.827 --> 00:16:00.662
When you smell things, it's
supposed to take you back

00:16:00.662 --> 00:16:02.738
and when I tell people,
when you smell wine,

00:16:02.738 --> 00:16:04.712
talk about what it makes you think about.

00:16:04.712 --> 00:16:06.244
Like, he was famous for saying,

00:16:06.244 --> 00:16:07.558
"This is like Big League Chew."

00:16:07.558 --> 00:16:08.949
Well, you know--
- 'Cause it was.

00:16:08.949 --> 00:16:09.924
- obviously it didn't taste like that,

00:16:09.924 --> 00:16:11.275
but, like, that's what it reminded him of.

00:16:11.275 --> 00:16:12.505
When you opened up that pouch,

00:16:12.505 --> 00:16:13.902
when you and your boys got into the store

00:16:13.902 --> 00:16:14.927
and you opened that pouch--
- That saccharin smell.

00:16:14.927 --> 00:16:16.866
- yeah, it's about memories,
like, someone's like,

00:16:16.866 --> 00:16:18.351
"Hey, this wine reminds
me of my grandfather,"

00:16:18.351 --> 00:16:20.199
and you're like, "So, what
about your grandfather?"

00:16:20.199 --> 00:16:21.411
"Yeah, I used to sit on his lap

00:16:21.411 --> 00:16:23.589
"and he would smoke a cigar," ah, right?

00:16:23.589 --> 00:16:26.248
It's the cedar, you're smelling
the oak on the wine, right?

00:16:26.248 --> 00:16:28.296
So, I think it's just more about memories.

00:16:28.296 --> 00:16:29.220
It's like when you

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:30.228
smell something--
- Mary Kate, I would

00:16:30.228 --> 00:16:32.295
add on that, like, to Andre's point, like,

00:16:32.295 --> 00:16:33.765
what really broke me out, I thought,

00:16:33.765 --> 00:16:36.397
with Wine Library TV, is that
I really just talked about,

00:16:36.397 --> 00:16:38.477
I remember being scared,

00:16:38.477 --> 00:16:40.759
to being called out in a blind tasting.

00:16:40.759 --> 00:16:42.379
I was in the wine world.

00:16:42.379 --> 00:16:44.145
It wasn't like I was
trying to be a comedian

00:16:44.145 --> 00:16:47.141
or doing a shtick, so,
I remember being fearful

00:16:47.141 --> 00:16:49.247
of people giving me a
glass of wine and saying,

00:16:49.247 --> 00:16:50.858
what do you smell, or what do you think?

00:16:50.858 --> 00:16:53.493
And so, I was like, look,
I need to go as true,

00:16:53.493 --> 00:16:54.587
for everybody who's listening,

00:16:54.587 --> 00:16:57.265
in the same way I kind of
call it like I see it stuff,

00:16:57.265 --> 00:17:00.722
I did that for wine, in
that I used Big League Chew,

00:17:00.722 --> 00:17:04.190
or the way a racketball smelled
to me, or anything else,

00:17:04.190 --> 00:17:08.247
because I knew that if I smelt
it again, I could recall it.

00:17:08.247 --> 00:17:11.088
And so, the nuances are there,

00:17:11.088 --> 00:17:14.578
as they are in coffee and
tea and other alcohols,

00:17:14.578 --> 00:17:17.795
brown goods, the flavors are there.

00:17:17.795 --> 00:17:21.120
I think to Andre's point,
and to your point, actually,

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:24.232
I apologize, there was a lot of adjectives

00:17:24.232 --> 00:17:26.689
and descriptors that were
established by the wine world,

00:17:26.689 --> 00:17:28.865
that everybody kept rinsing and repeating,

00:17:28.865 --> 00:17:31.789
and I feel like we
forced our ways into it.

00:17:31.789 --> 00:17:35.394
Whereas, now, I think,
with the democratization

00:17:35.394 --> 00:17:37.877
of Instagram and
personalities and bloggers

00:17:37.877 --> 00:17:39.911
and what I started with Wine Library TV,

00:17:39.911 --> 00:17:42.088
now that there aren't four or five people

00:17:42.088 --> 00:17:44.078
that control everything anymore,

00:17:44.078 --> 00:17:46.543
there's been more freedom
in the descriptors,

00:17:46.543 --> 00:17:47.376
and I think that's powerful.
- Absolutely, I mean,

00:17:47.376 --> 00:17:49.000
people describe wines as banging, right?

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:50.263
You're like, that one is banging.

00:17:50.263 --> 00:17:51.323
You're like, oh, wow, okay.

00:17:51.323 --> 00:17:52.958
- That makes sense--
- Yeah, see.

00:17:52.958 --> 00:17:53.994
- [Mary Kate] Yeah, it does, yeah.

00:17:53.994 --> 00:17:57.336
And also just the democratization
of the entry of wine too,

00:17:57.336 --> 00:18:00.198
the fact that you have
an OPP, I mean, like,

00:18:00.198 --> 00:18:03.042
that didn't exist 15 years ago.

00:18:03.042 --> 00:18:04.800
- All right, enough
bullshitting, let's drink this.

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:06.293
- All right.

00:18:06.293 --> 00:18:07.754
(slurping and swishing)

00:18:07.754 --> 00:18:10.719
- [Erin] How do you drink it, Andre?

00:18:10.719 --> 00:18:12.913
I love technical stuff like this.

00:18:12.913 --> 00:18:13.820
- [Gary] Carefully.

00:18:13.820 --> 00:18:15.942
- Yeah, very carefully,
is that what he said?

00:18:15.942 --> 00:18:17.290
Yeah, you just want to, you know, sip it,

00:18:17.290 --> 00:18:19.105
maybe pull in some air into your mouth.

00:18:19.105 --> 00:18:22.116
If you wanna gargle it, you can gargle it.

00:18:22.116 --> 00:18:24.266
All those things make a difference, like,

00:18:24.266 --> 00:18:25.963
some people swish it
around in their mouth,

00:18:25.963 --> 00:18:28.158
push it through the front
part of their teeth, you know,

